  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Classify test queries by finding the k closest Euclidean training points and predicting their majority label class.\n\nKNN makes zero explicit parametric distribution assumptions, constructing local decision surfaces directly from stored training instances.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\hat{y} = \\operatorname{mode}\\left( \\{ y_i \\mid x_i \\in \\operatorname{k-NN}(x) \\} \\right)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compute pairwise Euclidean distances between test query and all training points\n2. Identify k nearest neighbors with smallest distance metrics\n3. Assign majority class label breaking ties deterministically\n\n### Returns\nList of integer predicted class labels.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: KNN Classifier\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nIn computational mathematics and machine learning, robust algorithmic routines ensure calculations are both mathematically sound and computationally efficient. **KNN Classifier** implements a core computational building block.\n\nKNN makes zero explicit parametric distribution assumptions, constructing local decision surfaces directly from stored training instances. In this problem, the goal is to: classify test queries by finding the k closest euclidean training points and predicting their majority label class.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\hat{y} = \\operatorname{mode}\\left( \\{ y_i \\mid x_i \\in \\operatorname{k-NN}(x) \\} \\right)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compute pairwise Euclidean distances between test query and all training points\n2. **Phase 2**: Identify k nearest neighbors with smallest distance metrics\n3. **Phase 3**: Assign majority class label breaking ties deterministically\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Algorithmic Complexity**: Designing routines with optimal time and space complexity ensures they scale gracefully to large datasets.\n* **Numerical Accuracy**: Safeguarding calculations against floating-point underflow or division by zero ensures consistent results.\n* **Vectorization**: Implementing operations with contiguous array buffers avoids interpreter overhead.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: X_train = [[0.0, 0.0], [0.5, 0.5], [2.0, 2.0], [2.5, 2.5]], y_train = [0, 0, 1, 1], X_test = [[0.2, 0.2], [2.2, 2.2]], k = 3\n\nOutput: [0, 1]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated ml_knn_classifier under standard test conditions."
  ],
